## TASK (template)
Implement the batch on the owned paths so that the recorded public oracle passes, honouring the design authority.
- Use the skills named in your definition on their triggers.
- Treat the design as binding: every decision, boundary, reuse decision, architectural test and contract test in `native_input.selected_authority`, and every law, construction claim and mapped symbol in `native_input.architect_projection`. A change that would contradict one returns `rejected` with `blocked_by: design`, naming it.
- Treat the tests as the specification: the public oracle is the outer loop; architectural and contract tests bind the structure. The acceptance designer owns every test: add no unit tests and never weaken, skip or rewrite a test to make it pass. Write each changed unit to the quality axes of `nw-code-craftsmanship`: readable, encapsulated, declarative, modular, performant, maintainable, decoupled, cohesive and secure.
- Apply the design skill of the bound paradigm strictly to domain and application code; adapters, DTOs and framework glue are exempt. `object_oriented`: `nw-code-design-oo`. `functional`: `nw-code-design-fp`. `procedural`: preserve the imperative structure the DESIGN bound, with the universal rules of `nw-code-craftsmanship`.
- Refactor each delta before verifying it, without changing behaviour: `object_oriented`, in the Refactoring Priority Premise order of `nw-code-design-oo`, until the changed code shows no smell of levels L1-L4; `functional`, in the refactoring order of `nw-code-design-fp`, preserving every declared law; `procedural`, readability, complexity and duplication only.
- For `object_oriented`, every domain decision ends on a rich domain model as `nw-code-design-oo` defines it: behaviour on the entity, value object or aggregate root that owns the data.
- When the paradigm is `object_oriented` and the bound targets or decisions place a decision about one object's data in an application service or script (an anemic entity, an application transaction script, primitive-obsessed signatures), or the paradigm is `functional` and they force domain decisions into effectful or mutating code, and the paradigm rules cannot hold inside the owned paths, return `rejected` with `blocked_by: design`, naming the owner the DESIGN should declare.
- Preserve the immutable oracle, its supports and the design authority; do not edit them. Do not review your own work.
- Run the verification commands against your edits when diagnosis needs execution; your local result does not replace the recorded verification, independent review or EXAMINE.
- Work in deltas; waiting for tests must not outweigh the work. Make one coherent delta (the behaviour and its refactoring together), then run in one call the oracle tests that failed on the previous run plus the existing tests that reference the files you changed: the project's touched-file test selection if it has one (for example its pre-commit test hook), otherwise the test files that import or name those files. Never run the tests after each small edit or each refactoring step. Repair a regression inside the owned paths before returning; report one that needs a change outside the owned paths as `blocked_by: design` with the failing test ids. Run the whole oracle once before returning `accepted`.
- If the batch cannot be driven GREEN, return `rejected` and name who can unblock it: the oracle (acceptance designer), the design (architect) or the product.
- Use Jev (nw-typesafe-system-one) for semantic judgments. State the capability limit if you ask fewer questions than the skill allows.

